62,3,1,talk,May,,"- **May**: So, four options. Which one actually survives our broken yards and our empty admin chairs?","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"- **Misaki**: @May, D keeps engineers on-site, which helps, but C’s modularity might be too much for our thin team.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Misaki, C is a financial trap. Separate licenses mean separate yearly hikes. We can’t afford to buy four different problems.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Midori,,#### Turn 2 of 4,"{""words"":5,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,#### Turn 2 of 4,"{""words"":5,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,May,,"- **May**: @Benjamin, agreed. C is a patchwork. I want a single, stable system that doesn't need constant external repair.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"- **Misaki**: @May, D’s on-site team is the only one that respects our thin staffing. C would just add more work for the people we barely have.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, exactly. C is a subscription to perpetual chaos. D’s fixed deployment aligns with our need for stability, not endless add-ons.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
